1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,326
This is the Phase Two submission for our MCP academic assistant.

2
00:00:05,326 --> 00:00:14,042
The focus is validation and integration: realistic questions, independently checked answers, recorded measurements and evidence for the presentation.

3
00:00:14,042 --> 00:00:18,400
All records in this demonstration are generated sample data.

4
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:24,086
Phu and Hoang provide the academic features already on main.

5
00:00:24,086 --> 00:00:27,676
Tien’s finance work remains in pull request two.

6
00:00:27,676 --> 00:00:31,265
This branch contains Khoa’s questions and delivery artifacts.

7
00:00:31,265 --> 00:00:36,200
It does not merge or rewrite the other members’ server implementations.

8
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:39,870
A user asks a natural-language question.

9
00:00:39,870 --> 00:00:43,574
The host gives the model the enabled tool schemas.

10
00:00:43,574 --> 00:00:49,749
The model selects a tool and arguments, and the host calls the corresponding MCP server.

11
00:00:49,749 --> 00:00:55,100
The server calculates the result, and the model presents it to the user.

12
00:00:56,300 --> 00:01:00,473
The academic answer key is pinned to commit 2c4110a.

13
00:01:00,473 --> 00:01:05,109
It includes forty valid students, twenty teachers and forty-one sections.

14
00:01:05,109 --> 00:01:09,282
Six invalid grade rows are excluded from academic calculations.

15
00:01:09,282 --> 00:01:16,700
The manifest records the source commit and file hashes so the expected answers can be reproduced.

16
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:21,777
The test pack contains thirty-one natural English questions.

17
00:01:21,777 --> 00:01:24,200
Twenty-six can run against main.

18
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:31,954
Five finance cases preserve expected values from the finance pull request and are skipped on main.

19
00:01:31,954 --> 00:01:36,800
The workbook supplies fifty-nine formula metrics and independent CSV cross-checks.

20
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,688
The reference builder does not import the server’s business-rule functions.

21
00:01:42,688 --> 00:01:47,844
The workbook shows the CSV inputs, visible thresholds and formula steps.

22
00:01:47,844 --> 00:01:54,406
Every expected metric agrees with an independent CSV calculation under the artifact formula engine.

23
00:01:54,406 --> 00:02:00,500
A human review in Microsoft Excel is still required before claiming manual verification.

24
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:05,967
The first feature block covers teacher search and class schedules.

25
00:02:05,967 --> 00:02:09,380
Search supports several filters and a result limit.

26
00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:16,207
The timetable for class IT zero one in semester twenty twenty-four dash one contains three sections.

27
00:02:16,207 --> 00:02:20,900
A nonexistent class produces an error instead of a fabricated schedule.

28
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:25,316
Teacher workload counts assigned sections and weekly periods.

29
00:02:25,316 --> 00:02:31,748
T zero zero one has three sections and nine weekly periods in the first semester shown.

30
00:02:31,748 --> 00:02:35,366
A later semester has six sections and eighteen periods.

31
00:02:35,366 --> 00:02:42,200
A valid teacher with no assignments has a real zero workload, which differs from an unknown teacher.

32
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:46,229
Here is the recorded teacher workload question.

33
00:02:46,229 --> 00:02:53,504
The model called academic teacher workload with teacher T zero zero one and semester twenty twenty-four dash one.

34
00:02:53,504 --> 00:02:58,758
The returned result shows three assigned sections and nine teaching periods per week.

35
00:02:58,758 --> 00:03:02,800
The complete arguments and result remain in the raw trace.

36
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:08,750
The grade report aggregates graded enrolments across a teacher’s sections.

37
00:03:08,750 --> 00:03:11,600
It does not count unique students.

38
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:16,825
Each course total first uses the configured component weights and rounding.

39
00:03:16,825 --> 00:03:22,050
The report then averages those totals and calculates the pass percentage.

40
00:03:22,050 --> 00:03:24,900
The workbook preserves those intermediate calculations.

41
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:29,782
These two recorded attendance cases test the exact boundary.

42
00:03:29,782 --> 00:03:37,555
Three absences out of fifteen meetings equal twenty percent, so the strict greater-than rule does not ban the student.

43
00:03:37,555 --> 00:03:44,100
Four absences produce about twenty-six point seven percent and trigger an exam ban in that section.

44
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:50,008
This recorded case asks about four absences out of fifteen meetings.

45
00:03:50,008 --> 00:03:54,716
The model used both the student identifier and the section identifier.

46
00:03:54,716 --> 00:03:59,852
The result is approximately twenty-six point seven percent, with an exam ban.

47
00:03:59,852 --> 00:04:06,700
The companion case with three absences confirms that the exact twenty percent boundary is not banned.

48
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:12,965
Only the absent status currently contributes to the configured absence ratio.

49
00:04:12,965 --> 00:04:15,728
Late and excused attendance remain separate.

50
00:04:15,728 --> 00:04:22,635
The overall percentage summarizes a student’s records, but the exam restriction is evaluated per section.

51
00:04:22,635 --> 00:04:27,700
A low overall average can therefore hide a problematic individual section.

52
00:04:28,900 --> 00:04:31,715
Conduct classification comes from the rule file.

53
00:04:31,715 --> 00:04:38,952
The lower boundary belongs to the higher band: a score of fifty is Average, while ninety is Excellent.

54
00:04:38,952 --> 00:04:48,200
The test cases also cover a student’s history and a semester with no conduct record, which must remain unavailable rather than becoming zero.

55
00:04:49,400 --> 00:04:52,169
This trace shows the conduct boundary case.

56
00:04:52,169 --> 00:04:57,707
The tool returns a conduct score of fifty for the selected student and semester.

57
00:04:57,707 --> 00:04:59,289
The classification is Average.

58
00:04:59,289 --> 00:05:07,200
The model obtains the value from the tool, rather than inventing a score or confusing a missing record with zero.

59
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:13,392
The current risk tool flags a student when any configured condition applies.

60
00:05:13,392 --> 00:05:16,720
It does not require all three conditions together.

61
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:25,872
In this snapshot, two of ten students in IT zero one are flagged, and four of twenty in Business Administration are flagged.

62
00:05:25,872 --> 00:05:29,200
Reasons must explain each student’s actual risk factors.

63
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:35,025
The recorded class risk query evaluates ten students and flags two.

64
00:05:35,025 --> 00:05:37,548
The response contains the student-level reasons.

65
00:05:37,548 --> 00:05:48,900
A reviewer should compare those reasons with the complete structured results, because a numeric count alone does not prove that the final answer preserved every risk explanation.

66
00:05:50,100 --> 00:05:53,034
The finance feature is a separate dependency.

67
00:05:53,034 --> 00:06:00,160
Its human-resources and conduct data differ from main, so mixing the two snapshots would change expected values.

68
00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:03,094
The workbook keeps finance source sheets separate.

69
00:06:03,094 --> 00:06:09,800
The five finance cases are ready for review but are not reported as executed on main.

70
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,736
This tuition example is calculated from the finance pull request’s sample CSV.

71
00:06:15,736 --> 00:06:20,077
The visible formula subtracts the paid amount from the amount due.

72
00:06:20,077 --> 00:06:25,996
Student twenty-two zero one zero zero two has an unpaid balance in the selected semester.

73
00:06:25,996 --> 00:06:33,100
This slide explains the reference calculation; it is not a claim that the finance tool ran on main.

74
00:06:34,300 --> 00:06:40,567
Teacher pay combines the payroll row with teaching periods from the matching finance snapshot.

75
00:06:40,567 --> 00:06:46,386
Title rates, weeks per month and the overtime threshold come from finance configuration.

76
00:06:46,386 --> 00:06:49,967
The workbook calculates each component before net pay.

77
00:06:49,967 --> 00:06:53,100
These are fictional payroll records for testing.

78
00:06:54,300 --> 00:06:56,724
The advising scenario combines several sources.

79
00:06:56,724 --> 00:07:03,592
The model must obtain the transcript, attendance summary and conduct score for the same student and semester.

80
00:07:03,592 --> 00:07:06,016
The trace records the tool calls.

81
00:07:06,016 --> 00:07:14,500
A human reviewer must still check that the final briefing includes every relevant restriction and does not lose a risk reason.

82
00:07:15,700 --> 00:07:19,073
The final recorded scenario is an advising briefing.

83
00:07:19,073 --> 00:07:25,396
The model calls the transcript, attendance and conduct tools with the same student and semester.

84
00:07:25,396 --> 00:07:29,612
The answer combines academic performance with exam restrictions and conduct.

85
00:07:29,612 --> 00:07:32,984
This is a replay of the measured response.

86
00:07:32,984 --> 00:07:37,200
It is not a fresh model request during video playback.

87
00:07:38,400 --> 00:07:46,133
The comparison changes the transport while keeping the model, temperature, tool-round limit and system prompt fixed.

88
00:07:46,133 --> 00:07:49,033
Each question starts a fresh conversation.

89
00:07:49,033 --> 00:07:54,350
Fallback is disabled so every model call has an unambiguous attribution.

90
00:07:54,350 --> 00:07:58,700
Both configurations enable only the academic and utility servers.

91
00:07:59,900 --> 00:08:06,868
The independent numeric subset checks passed all twenty-six executable academic cases over both transports.

92
00:08:06,868 --> 00:08:11,348
These direct tests do not use a language model.

93
00:08:11,348 --> 00:08:18,316
They verify the MCP route, expected argument handling, numeric outputs and expected error behavior.

94
00:08:18,316 --> 00:08:21,800
The five finance cases remain explicitly skipped.

95
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,834
These are the recorded Gemini results.

96
00:08:25,834 --> 00:08:32,447
The strict subset score checks requested tools, required argument values and selected structured values.

97
00:08:32,447 --> 00:08:36,226
It is not a complete semantic answer score.

98
00:08:36,226 --> 00:08:45,200
Latency includes API retry time within each question, so a slow outlier must not automatically be attributed to HTTP.

99
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:52,165
The token totals come from the recorded provider usage for these twenty-six questions.

100
00:08:52,165 --> 00:08:56,157
They include repeated tool schemas and the conversation rounds.

101
00:08:56,157 --> 00:09:00,148
The model receives more than the user’s question alone.

102
00:09:00,148 --> 00:09:06,800
A complete three-server context-budget experiment still requires the finance integration and its actual tool list.

103
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:11,098
The limit test exposes a useful distinction.

104
00:09:11,098 --> 00:09:21,277
A model may retrieve fifty teachers and then show one person, satisfying much of the visible question while ignoring the intended retrieval limit.

105
00:09:21,277 --> 00:09:23,047
That increases unnecessary context.

106
00:09:23,047 --> 00:09:28,800
The report preserves the strict argument failure and leaves semantic correctness for review.

107
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:38,656
One sweep per transport is enough to report what happened, but not enough to establish a general speed ranking.

108
00:09:38,656 --> 00:09:41,844
Retries, network conditions and warm-up all matter.

109
00:09:41,844 --> 00:09:45,033
Repeated randomized sweeps are the next step.

110
00:09:45,033 --> 00:09:50,500
Historical model-comparison results from another machine must remain separate from these measurements.

111
00:09:51,700 --> 00:09:58,342
The submission adds an independent reference, full tool-result capture, source hashes and fresh conversations.

112
00:09:58,342 --> 00:10:01,663
These changes make failures easier to diagnose.

113
00:10:01,663 --> 00:10:12,100
The original model observations and improvement log remain useful context, but their historical scores are not relabelled as new Phase Two measurements.

114
00:10:13,300 --> 00:10:26,647
The remaining gates are explicit: human review of the Excel key and final answers, finance integration with a refreshed reference, a local Qwen run when Ollama is available, and a genuine tool-description experiment.

115
00:10:26,647 --> 00:10:31,500
A terse host prompt alone does not shorten the registered tool schemas.

116
00:10:32,700 --> 00:10:36,572
The backup video is a narrated replay of recorded evidence.

117
00:10:36,572 --> 00:10:42,381
It shows selected questions, tool arguments and observed answers, with English synthetic narration and subtitles.

118
00:10:42,381 --> 00:10:50,900
It is labelled as a replay so it cannot be mistaken for a fresh live run or a recording spoken by Khoa.

119
00:10:52,100 --> 00:11:02,379
The delivery contains the test CSV, formula workbook, configuration snapshots, raw traces, comparison tables, editable slides, quiz and backup video.

120
00:11:02,379 --> 00:11:06,491
The pull request isolates Khoa’s work from main.

121
00:11:06,491 --> 00:11:14,200
The handoff guide explains reproduction and identifies the review steps that still require the team.
